  <h2>What is AOSP?</h2>
  <p>
    The Android Open Source Project is the open-source project, led by Google, that
    publishes the Android operating system stack — the layers shown above: apps,
    the Android framework, native libraries, the Android runtime, the hardware
    abstraction layer, and the Linux kernel. Device makers and ROM developers
    download this code, adapt it to their hardware, and ship it.
  </p>

  <h2>What AOSP is not</h2>
  <p>
    Google's proprietary pieces — the Play Store, Google Play services, Gmail, and
    friends — are <em>not</em> part of AOSP. What ships on a retail phone is AOSP
    plus the manufacturer's and Google's additions. That gap is exactly what custom
    ROMs exploit: they are built from AOSP source with their own choices on top.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters for rooting and ROMs</h2>
  <p>
    Every custom ROM begins as AOSP source code — LineageOS,
    <a href="/graphene-os">GrapheneOS</a>, and the rest all build on it. When
    you're weighing a ROM for your device, the AOSP version it tracks tells you a
    lot about what you're getting. See our <a href="/roms">ROM catalog</a> for
    what's out there, or <a href="/scan">scan your device</a> to find out what it
    runs today.
  </p>
